
Dihedral group D_12 = ⟨r, s | r^6 = s^2 = 1, s r s = r^{-1}⟩ (order 12), written as permutations of Z/6:
r: i ↦ i+1, and reflections f_c: i ↦ c − i (c ∈ Z/6), f_0 = s.
Exact covering by 6 left cosets:
  {e}                 = coset of the trivial subgroup           index 12
  {f_1, f_3, f_5}     = f_1·⟨r^2⟩                               index 4
  {f_0, r^3}          = f_0·⟨f_3⟩      (f_0 f_3 = r^3)          index 6
  {r,   f_4}          = r·⟨f_3⟩        (r f_3 = f_4)            index 6
  {f_2, r^4}          = f_2·⟨f_4⟩      (f_2 f_4 = r^{-2} = r^4) index 6
  {r^2, r^5}          = r^2·⟨r^3⟩                               index 6
Indices (12, 4, 6, 6, 6, 6): the LARGEST index 12 occurs exactly once, so both the Mirsky–Newman form
('the two largest indices coincide') and the Znám form ('the largest index occurs ≥ p(n_k) times') of HS FAIL in D_12,
while HS itself (some index repeated) holds. Found by exhaustive enumeration (hs_znam.py); verified by direct
coset checks (see hs_search.Group). Among the groups tested, every group of order ≤ 11 and every other group of
order 12 (C12, C2×C6, A4, Dic3) satisfies the strong form, so D_12 is the smallest group where the strong form fails
(groups of order < 12 are nilpotent or dihedral of order 6 or 10, all tested; nilpotent groups satisfy the strong
form by Berger–Felzenbaum–Fraenkel / Sun). Consistent with Sun (2004), whose strong form needs the G_i subnormal:
the order-2 subgroups ⟨f_c⟩ of D_12 are not subnormal.


